WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:13.580
The EURO is to currencies what TINDER is to Apps: everybody loves to criticise it but…

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.830
at the end of the day, a lot of people you
wouldn’t expect end up using it.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:22.520
I’m sure many of you have heard economists
and experts of all kinds making predictions

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.090
about the end of the single European currency.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:34.200
The best thing Germany could do for Europe
is quit the single currency – but it won’t

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.570
Larry Elliott for The Guardian

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:42.680
“The euro is a patently flawed construct,
which its architects knew at the time of its

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.510
creation” –George Soros

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.340
Nevertheless, the reality is way different.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.750
In fact, the EURO is the second most common
worldwide reserve currency.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:59.239
And at the end of this video, you’ll see
how it might not be far from being the first.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:02.170
Think I’m going nuts?

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.809
Definitely not!

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:09.290
Look, one third of the International trade
uses the EURO as its exchange currency.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:13.720
20% of the reserve currencies from central
banks are denominated in Euros.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:21.900
And many other countries also issue debt in
this currency.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.870
But this is not the end of the story!

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:28.120
If you follow VisualPolitik every week, you
already know that Donald Trump is putting

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.650
lots of tariffs on international trade…

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.390
So what does this mean?

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:33.390
Think about it!

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:38.340
If Americans are going to buy less stuff abroad,
there will be fewer dollars in circulation.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.340
Then again…

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.150
Who’s signing free trade deals with every
mother’s son?

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.150
Exactly!

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:45.150
The European Union!

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:48.320
They have already passed a treaty with Canada,
the so called CETA.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.630
But this is not the only one!

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.790
In the last two years, Europe has reduced
tariffs with Japan, Mexico and many other

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.200
countries.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:04.640
In other words, the more Europeans buy abroad,
the more EUROS there will be in circulation.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.780
This means there could be a moment where instead
of having ONE hegemonic reserve currency…

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.200
we might have TWO: the US Dollar and the Euro.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.700
OK, OK, I know what you’re thinking now…
and what good does this do?

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.639
Is it really useful for Europeans to have
the EURO as the hegemonic currency?

00:02:22.639 --> 00:02:26.480
Well… yes and, at the same time… no.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.180
If you’re watching this video from any EU
country and, let’s say, you have a company,

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:38.090
having the EURO as the World’s reserve currency
won’t help you all that much.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.269
But… if you’re working for a European
Government…

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:45.700
Boy, you are really feeling lucky!!!

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.060
If there’s a big demand for euros, the European
Central Bank will have a bigger margin to

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.209
print money without devaluing it.

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:55.659
Anyway, we talk about this in our video about
the dollar.

00:02:55.659 --> 00:03:01.569
If you haven’t seen it yet, there’s a
link in the description.

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:03.099
Now the big question is…

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:06.639
Why is the Euro more popular than any other
currency?

00:03:06.639 --> 00:03:10.090
For example… why the Euro and not the Chinese
Yuan?

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:13.340
Why are so many economists so critical of
the euro?

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.290
Today, we are going to answer these questions,
but before we do, let’s take a look back

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:21.230
at history.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.469
EXISTENTIAL CRISIS

00:03:24.469 --> 00:03:28.069
During the 70s, European countries were happy
to trade with each other.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:32.680
Although there was no European Union back
then, there was something very similar, the

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.140
so-called COMMON MARKET.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.900
Nonetheless, there was a problem.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.819
Each country had its own national currency.

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:40.879
And each currency worked in a different way.

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:43.480
For example, the German Mark was strong and
stable.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.709
But the Italian lira was devaluing every other
day.

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:48.069
So imagine this situation.

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:53.069
You work for a German bank and you lend 1
million liras to an Italian company.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:57.400
In this moment, a million liras are worth
1000 German marks.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.510
But when it’s time for that Italian company
to give you back the money, the Lira has been

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:02.510
devalued.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:06.489
So, even if you get 1 million liras back,
when you exchange it to your local currency,

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:09.400
you only get, say, 900 german marks.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:14.980
A hell of a business transaction!

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:16.840
Isn’t it?

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:23.210
OK, so in order to avoid this, the European
politicians met in a room and… well…

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.250
…apparently they tried something… pretty
strong.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:29.320
They came to an agreement called the... snake...
in the... tunnel.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.039
So, well, the snake is kind of a methaphor
for the currency fluctuations... right?

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:36.090
Because... you know, currencies go up and
down like a snake.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.250
So, if you don’t want the snake to go crazy...
you...

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.740
make a tunnel, right?

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.000
This way... the currency doesn’t fluctuate.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.261
Well... look my dear viewers: this was the
70s, a crazy time were Bob Marley was still

00:04:48.261 --> 00:04:54.650
alive and bellbottoms were trendy.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:05:02.180
So did this snake in the tunnel agreement
work?

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.659
Of course not!

00:05:03.659 --> 00:05:09.200
During the 80s, European currencies went up
and down like a... drunk snake in a very wide

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.849
tunnel.

00:05:12.849 --> 00:05:17.220
This is why, decades later, European politicians
met again in another room.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:23.960
This time it was 1992 and this agreement had
a more proper name: They called it the MAASTRICH

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:24.960
treaty.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.270
And in this one, they got really serious and
said:

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:29.759
Let’s not fuck around!

00:05:29.759 --> 00:05:34.730
We will create a common currency for all.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:39.090
No more exchange rates, fluctuations, or bullshit...

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.090
What?

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:41.090
What do you say, Great Britain?

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:42.419
You don’t want in?

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:43.419
OK!

00:05:43.419 --> 00:05:44.419
Great Britain!

00:05:44.419 --> 00:05:48.900
You can go fuck yourself with your sterling
pound, your five o’clock tea, and your driving

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.039
on the wrong side of the road!!!‘

00:05:52.039 --> 00:05:58.020
And this is how a three step process to create
a single currency started.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:08.060
In 1999, all the EU stock markets started
listing in Euros and, later on, European citizens

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:11.260
started using the new coins for their daily
trades.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:15.349
Now we all have gotten used to hearing about
Euros like any other normal currency.

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:20.759
But make no mistake, back then, it was an
unprecedented financial experiment.

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:25.880
And... this is how the first critics came
up.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:32.400
Europe exemplifies a situation unfavourable
to a common currency.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:37.569
It is composed of separate nations, speaking
different languages, with different customs,

00:06:37.569 --> 00:06:43.439
and having citizens feeling far greater loyalty
and attachment to their own country than to

00:06:43.439 --> 00:06:46.389
a common market or to the idea of Europe.

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:48.650
–Milton Friedman
And now you might wonder…

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.720
What is the problem with a single currency?

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:51.879
That’s easy.

00:06:51.879 --> 00:06:56.080
If we have a single currency, that German
bank we talked about before, will have no

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:59.639
problems lending money to an Italian or a
Greek company.

00:06:59.639 --> 00:07:00.849
So what happens, then?

00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:05.419
Well… since there is no currency risk, German
banks, having a lot of money, lend it to poorer

00:07:05.419 --> 00:07:07.629
Italian, Greek and Spanish companies.

00:07:07.629 --> 00:07:12.919
All of a sudden, rich countries started investing
in poor countries like there was no tomorrow.

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:15.759
And yes, yes, I know what you’re thinking
now…

00:07:15.759 --> 00:07:16.810
Where’s the problem, then?

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:27.580
Well, the problem comes with the crisis.

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:32.389
Banks must take bigger losses on Greek loans,
says German finance minister

00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:36.419
Basically, the Southern European economies
are poorer and weaker.

00:07:36.419 --> 00:07:39.550
When there’s a crisis, their companies are
the first ones to go bankrupt.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:43.229
So if companies can’t pay their debts back,
the banks can go bankrupt too.

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:46.940
This is what we call the RISK OF CONTAGION.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:51.479
This explains why, during the 2008 crisis,
so many politicians and economists suggested

00:07:51.479 --> 00:07:53.180
abandoning the Euro.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:57.240
Many of them talked about the creation of
two different EUROS: one for the wealthy countries,

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:01.740
solid and stable, and another for the poor
ones.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:05.330
One that could be severely devalued.

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:10.259
Nonetheless… today, the EURO is still alive
and kicking.

00:08:10.259 --> 00:08:11.719
Why?

00:08:11.719 --> 00:08:17.569
We’ll see right now.

00:08:17.569 --> 00:08:21.960
LONG LIVE CHAOS!!!

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:28.300
Critics are right: the European Central Bank,
also known as the ECB, is a modern day Babel

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:29.300
Tower.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:35.450
It works similarly to a country’s government.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:39.370
The current president is a man named Mario
Dragui and the executive board is something

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.080
like the executive power.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:45.320
But we also have a legislative power: the
Governing Council.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:49.270
This is where you have representatives from
all the Eurozone countries.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:53.320
There’s one from Spain, one from France,
Germany, Slovenia… well, all the countries

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.700
that have the euro as their currency.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.030
These guys are the real decision makers.

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:02.830
They decide whether to raise of lower interest
rates or print more or less money.

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:06.210
But before they do, they have to reach an
agreement.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:08.400
And this is where the problems start!

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:13.130
The Southern European countries want to print
more money but their Northern neighbours don’t.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:22.970
Since they can’t come to an agreement, it’s
almost impossible to make drastic decisions.

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:25.270
This is all true and the problem is…

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.170
(((LOOK AT THE CAMERA FOR 4 SECONDS IN PURE
SILENCE)))

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:28.170
… ahm… well…

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:29.170
Where is the problem?

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:33.070
I mean, where is the problem in having a currency
that cannot be devalued because those who

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:36.070
are in charge of printing it don’t agree
with each other?

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:37.480
Isn’t this a strength?

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:40.500
We talked about this thousands of times on
VisualPolitik.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.440
The best currency is the strongest one.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:46.680
If the value of your money doesn’t change,
there is low inflation and you can do your

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.880
shopping without being afraid of losing your
money.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.980
You can start your tech company because you
know you can invest long term.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:58.650
And you might wonder now… but is the EURO
that stable?

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:03.070
Well… look at this chart!

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:10.780
Here you can see a comparison between the
monetary supply, the so called M2, of the

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:13.330
ECB and the American Federal Reserve.

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.790
Can you see how they go hand in hand?

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:19.880
In less than 20 years, the EURO has become
as solid as the American Dollar.

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:23.660
In fact, Europeans have already forgotten
what inflation even is.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:28.560
For a better understanding, the American Federal
Reserve has two main goals: keeping inflation

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.150
low and keeping unemployment low too.

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:36.140
The European Central Bank, instead, has just
one goal on their agenda: monetary stability.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:38.960
And, so far, they have achieved it!

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:46.090
So yes, we can say that the EURO is as solid
and abundant as the USD but… could it be

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:49.600
even more solid and abundant?

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:52.370
Well… the answer is yes.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:58.100
Today, Eurozone has around 320 million inhabitants…
almost the same as the United States.

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:04.010
But despite the European economy being poorer
than the American one, the eurozone can increase

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:08.170
as long as new countries join it.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:15.230
In the last 10 years, 6 new countries have
adopted the Euro as their currency.

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:21.610
And, despite the political climate not being
very in favour of it, Poland of the Nordic

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:25.830
countries might end up using it at some point.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:29.790
If this wasn’t enough, there are many other
currencies that are linked to the EURO.

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:33.520
Do you remember this other video where we
talked about the French colonies in Africa?

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:38.180
There we said how countries like IVORY COAST
and SENEGAL have a common currency called

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:39.530
the CFA FRANC.

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:42.420
This currency is linked to the EURO.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:46.880
And we’re talking about more than 150 million
people in emerging economies.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:51.120
In other words, with so many people using
EUROS in their daily lives, there are enough

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.790
coins out there to make it easy for any company
to get them.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:56.660
But, OK, now you might be thinking…

00:11:56.660 --> 00:12:01.380
Yes, Simon, a lot of people are using Euros
but… you know what other currency has a

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:02.380
lot users?

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:04.480
The Chinese Yuan!

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:05.590
Also known as the RENMINBI.

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:09.220
It’s becoming more and more in fashion nowadays…
so…

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:13.360
Why are we gonna use EUROS and not RENMIMBIS?

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:16.940
Well, we’ll see why right now.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:18.140
YUAN’S FLAWS

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:20.450
You’re right!

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:21.690
China is full of Chinese people!

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:25.130
And those Chinese people use YUANS, also known
as RENMIMBIS, for their daily transactions.

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:30.130
This means high great demand for this currency
and lots of notes and coins in the market.

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:34.420
At first glance, this should be enough for
the YUAN to become an international reserve

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:35.420
currency, right?

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:39.220
Well… there is a problem…

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:46.470
China puts US$15,000 annual personal cap on
overseas bank card withdrawals

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:51.220
Yes, you heard this right: China does not
want yuans to leave the country.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.900
This is why they put lots of capital controls
in place.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:57.920
In other words, finding yuans in the international
market is not an easy job.

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:02.260
In fact, China wants to avoid Chinese businessmen
taking Money out of the country.

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:05.360
Hence, the Chinese People’s Bank has lots
of restrictions.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:10.280
And, as you can imagine, in a dictatorship
like the Chinese one, it’s hard to believe

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:12.260
in the independence of their central bank.

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:16.100
In other words, if Xi Jimping needed to, he
could change the Governor’s bank and start

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:18.340
printing money at will.

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:20.750
Don’t believe me?

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:25.140
Do you really think a political system doesn’t
affect a currency?

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:26.140
Oh, come on!

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.170
You should watch our video on Turkey.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:32.950
There you’ll see what happens when you mix
authoritarianism and economic crisis.

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:34.790
Meanwhile, it’s your turn.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:38.800
Do you think the EURO can become as important
as the dollar?

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:43.110
Or maybe we’ll see a brand new crisis destroying
the European single currency?

